course: dictionary
lesson: 14069
title: One word, many meanings: maul
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. «The bear began to maul the tent.» The word bear and the attack on the tent point to claws and teeth, so maul means to attack and hurt badly.
explain: «Our team was mauled 9-0 on Saturday.» No animal is involved here, so the meaning must change. Mauled means beaten very heavily, which is an informal way of talking about sport. Always ask: does my first meaning still make sense in this sentence?
explain: Maul can also be a noun, a thing rather than an action. «The players formed a maul around the ball.» Look at the small words before it: a and the point to a noun, while to and will point to a verb.
fact: Maul is both a verb (an action word) and a noun (a naming word), and the context shows which one it is.
fact: «Maul» means to attack and hurt badly with claws and teeth, as a bear or big cat might.
fact: «Maul» can also mean to handle something roughly, as in «the toddler mauled the cake».
fact: In informal speech, «mauled» means beaten very heavily or criticised strongly.
fact: In rugby, a maul is a group of players pushing together around the player holding the ball. A maul can also be a heavy hammer for splitting wood.
exercise: «The hungry tiger began to maul the deer.» What does maul mean here? | To paint a picture slowly | To sing a happy song | *To attack and hurt badly with claws and teeth
exercise: «Our team was mauled 9-0 by the top club.» What does mauled mean here? | Cooked in a pan | Taken for a walk | *Beaten very heavily | Given a gift
exercise: «The players formed a maul around the ball.» What is a maul in rugby? | A short speech before the game | *A group of players pushing together around the player with the ball | A kind of shoe | A loud whistle from the referee
test: «The toddler mauled the cake with sticky hands.» What does mauled mean here? | *To handle it roughly | To bake carefully | To eat it slowly
test: Which sentence uses maul as a noun? | The team will maul their rivals on Friday. | The dog began to maul the slipper. | *The workman swung a maul to split the log.
exam: In «The critics mauled the new film», what does maul mean? | To watch carefully | *To criticise it strongly | To paint it on a wall | To pack it in a box
